-- 043: rework the bilingual MVP / Prototype / PoC Insight into a complete editorial decision guide.
-- This is a new migration. Migrations 039-042 remain immutable.

BEGIN;

UPDATE cms_insights
SET title='MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Which One Should Your Business Choose?', excerpt='Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept reduce different kinds of uncertainty. Learn what each validates, what it cannot prove, how to compare them, and how to choose the smallest credible experiment for your biggest product risk.', content='[{"type":"paragraph","text":"When a company wants to build a new digital product, the conversation often jumps straight to features, technology, timelines, and budget. Those things matter, but they do not answer the first question that should shape the work: what could still make the product fail?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"That uncertainty may be about the experience, the value of the product, or the technology underneath it. A team can have a convincing business idea and still not know whether users will understand the workflow. It can have a strong prototype and still not know whether real customers will adopt it. It can also have a clear product direction while a critical integration, data source, or performance requirement remains unproven."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept are often treated as three sizes of the same product. They are not. Each is a validation instrument designed to produce a different kind of evidence."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The most useful mental model in this article is simple: **choose the smallest thing that can answer your biggest uncertainty**. That principle keeps design and engineering focused on decisions instead of deliverables."},{"type":"callout","title":"The principle","text":"Do not choose a method because the label sounds more advanced. Choose it because it can reduce the risk that matters most right now.","tone":"info"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"The 30-second answer"},{"type":"table","headers":["Approach","Best for","Main question","Typical evidence"],"rows":[["Prototype","Experience risk","Can users understand and use the experience?","Observed usability, comprehension, task success, feedback"],["MVP","Product-value risk","Will real users adopt and value the core solution?","Usage, activation, retention, conversion, operational behavior"],["Proof of Concept","Technical risk","Can the critical technical approach work?","Integration results, performance, accuracy, reliability, constraints"]]},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/validate.svg","alt":"Prototype validates user experience, MVP validates product value, and Proof of Concept validates technical feasibility","caption":"The three approaches are different because the questions are different: experience, product value, and technical feasibility."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"There is no universal rule that says every product should move through Prototype, then PoC, then MVP. The right order depends on which unanswered question could cause the most expensive mistake."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What Is a Prototype?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A Prototype is a representation of a proposed experience that is detailed enough to test how people understand, navigate, and complete key tasks. It can be a low-fidelity sketch, a clickable flow, or a high-fidelity interface. The important part is not the visual polish. The important part is whether it lets you observe the behavior you are uncertain about."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What does a Prototype validate?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype testing is most useful when the uncertainty sits in information hierarchy, navigation, interaction, workflow, wording, or task completion. Nielsen Norman Group describes prototype testing as usability testing performed on a representation of a product before the live product exists, which makes it useful for identifying problems before implementation cost rises. Nielsen Norman Group describes prototype testing as usability testing on a representation of a product before the live product exists. See [NN/g''s prototype testing guidance](https://media.nngroup.com/media/articles/attachments/glossary-ux-research.pdf)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A good prototype therefore gives the team evidence about whether users understand the intended flow, where they hesitate, what they expect next, and which parts of the experience need to change."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What does a Prototype not prove?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A convincing prototype does not automatically prove market demand, production reliability, backend architecture, security, or technical scalability. It can show that an experience makes sense to a user without proving that the full system can support it."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"When should you build one?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Core workflows are still changing.","Users may interpret the interface or process differently from the team.","Stakeholders disagree about how the product should work.","The cost of changing the experience after development would be high.","You need to compare alternatives before choosing an implementation direction."]},{"type":"callout","title":"Useful rule","text":"If the biggest risk is “we may be designing the wrong experience,” a Prototype is usually a better first experiment than a full MVP.","tone":"neutral"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What Is an MVP?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A Minimum Viable Product is a working product that is deliberately scoped to test a meaningful value hypothesis with real users. Its purpose is not simply to ship a smaller product. Its purpose is to generate validated learning with enough real usage to inform the next product decision."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Eric Ries frames an MVP around validated learning: the team builds the minimum version needed to learn whether a product hypothesis is working. That means the scope should follow the learning goal, not a fixed feature count."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/learning-loop.svg","alt":"MVP learning loop from problem and core solution to real users, feedback, learning, and iteration","caption":"An MVP is a learning loop. The product is the instrument used to collect evidence and decide what to build next."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What does an MVP validate?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Whether the core value proposition leads to meaningful user behavior.","Whether users activate, complete the key task, return, or convert.","Whether the product solves a problem strongly enough to change an existing behavior.","Which parts of the workflow create value and which parts are unnecessary."]},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What does an MVP not automatically prove?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An MVP can provide strong evidence of value while still needing more work for enterprise security, reliability, scale, operational tooling, accessibility, or broader feature coverage. Production readiness is a separate decision from whether the core value deserves continued investment."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"When should you build one?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Build an MVP when the problem and target user are clear enough that real usage can answer a value question. If the team still does not know whether users can understand the basic workflow, testing that experience first may be more efficient."},{"type":"callout","title":"Remember","text":"An MVP should be as small as possible **without becoming too small to produce trustworthy evidence**. The goal is not minimum code. The goal is minimum credible learning.","tone":"info"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What Is a Proof of Concept?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A Proof of Concept, or PoC, is a focused technical experiment used to determine whether a critical approach is feasible enough to continue. It is most valuable when one technical unknown could invalidate a broader product plan."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Typical PoC questions include whether a legacy API can deliver the required data within an acceptable latency, whether an external service can support the required integration pattern, whether an AI model reaches a minimum accuracy threshold on a real dataset, or whether a particular architecture can meet a known constraint."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What makes a PoC useful?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["A single, explicit technical question.","A deliberately narrow scope.","Clear success and failure criteria.","A measurable test condition.","A decision attached to the result."]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Guidance from digital.gov.au treats a PoC as a small experiment used to validate feasibility before greater investment. That distinction matters: a successful PoC proves the tested technical assumption, not that the whole product is ready to launch. IBM similarly recommends focused experimentation when evaluating new technologies and their fit with existing systems. Guidance from [digital.gov.au](https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/AI-POC-to-scale/context-and-principles) treats a PoC as a focused experiment for validating feasibility before larger investment. [IBM](https://www.ibm.com/think/insights/guide-to-experimenting-new-ai-technologies) similarly recommends focused experimentation when evaluating new technologies and their fit with existing systems."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"When should you build one?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use a PoC when the technical unknown is both important and expensive to discover late. If the underlying technology is already proven and the bigger uncertainty is user value or usability, a PoC may add little value."},{"type":"quote","text":"A successful PoC answers “can this work under the tested conditions?” It does not answer “should we build the entire product?”"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"The Real Difference Between Prototype, MVP and PoC"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The clearest distinction is not the artifact itself. It is the evidence you need after the experiment. A prototype may be built in design software, an MVP may include production code, and a PoC may contain almost no polished interface. What matters is the question each artifact is designed to answer."},{"type":"table","headers":["Dimension","Prototype","MVP","PoC"],"rows":[["Primary purpose","Validate experience","Validate product value","Validate technical feasibility"],["Main question","Can users use it?","Will users value it?","Can we build it?"],["Primary risk","User experience","Adoption and value","Technical failure"],["Real users","Useful, not always required","Central to the test","Usually not the main audience"],["Functional depth","Mostly simulated","Core product behavior works","Only the tested technical path needs to work"],["UX fidelity","Low → High","Medium → High","Low unless the interface itself is the test"],["Technical depth","Low → Medium","Medium → High","High in the tested area"],["Business validation","Limited","High","Limited"],["Technical validation","Limited","Partial","High"],["Production-ready","No","Not automatically","No"],["Typical output","Tested experience","Evidence from real usage","Technical proof and constraints"],["Best next decision","Change the experience","Continue, change, or stop the product direction","Proceed, redesign, change technology, or stop"]]},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/comparison.svg","alt":"Comparison of Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept across purpose, evidence, UX fidelity, technical depth, and readiness","caption":"Prototype, MVP, and PoC optimize for different evidence. None of them is a universal shortcut to a production-ready product."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Feasibility, value, and readiness are three different questions"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A product can be technically feasible and still have no meaningful demand. Users can love a workflow that is too expensive or unreliable to operate. A working MVP can demonstrate value while still requiring substantial hardening before it becomes an enterprise-grade system."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Keeping these questions separate prevents a common failure mode: treating evidence from one experiment as proof of everything else."},{"type":"callout","title":"Need help choosing the right starting point?","text":"Kapitech can help map the product risk, user needs, and technical constraints before you commit to a larger build.","tone":"neutral"},{"type":"link","label":"Discuss Your Product With Kapitech","url":"https://kapitech.id/contact"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"How to Choose the Right Approach"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Start with the uncertainty that could change the project decision. Do not start by asking which deliverable the team feels most comfortable producing."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/decision-tree.svg","alt":"Decision tree for choosing Prototype, MVP, or Proof of Concept based on the biggest uncertainty","caption":"Start with the risk that could change the project decision. Then select the smallest credible experiment."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"1. Write the uncertainty as a question"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Turn “the product is risky” into something testable: Can users find the approval status without training? Will operations teams use the workflow every week? Can the legacy API return the required data within the response time we need?"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"2. Identify the cost of being wrong"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Not every unknown deserves the same investment. Prioritize the one that could create the largest rework, wasted engineering effort, or incorrect product decision."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"3. Choose the smallest credible experiment"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Select a Prototype, MVP, PoC, or combination that can generate evidence without solving unrelated problems. The smallest experiment is not the one with the fewest pixels or lines of code. It is the one with the smallest scope that still produces credible evidence."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"4. Define the evidence before you start"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Decide what you will observe, what counts as strong enough evidence, and what would make you change direction. A test becomes much more useful when the decision threshold exists before the result arrives."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"5. Attach a decision to the result"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Every validation experiment should lead to a next action: continue, change, test again, or stop. Otherwise, the team may collect information without reducing uncertainty."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"A Real Business Scenario: B2B Operations Platform"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Imagine a company wants to build a B2B operations platform for approvals, workflow management, operational dashboards, reporting, and notifications. The same product can require all three validation methods, but not for the same reason."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Question A: Will people understand the workflow?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The team is unsure whether operations users can understand the approval sequence, identify the current status, and recover from exceptions. A Prototype is appropriate because the primary uncertainty is experience."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Question B: Will the product create enough value to change behavior?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The flow is understandable, but the team has no evidence that users will return to the system or that the new workflow will replace the current manual process. An MVP is appropriate because the question is about real adoption and value."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Question C: Can the legacy API support the experience?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The planned dashboard depends on an older system that was never designed for the new usage pattern. A PoC can isolate the API integration, response time, data completeness, or concurrency question before the team commits to the full implementation."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is why the methods should not be treated as a mandatory staircase. One project may need a Prototype and PoC in parallel. Another may need only an MVP. The sequence should follow the risk, not the label."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Common Mistakes When Choosing Between MVP, Prototype and PoC"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"1. Building too much before asking the key question"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Teams often add settings, roles, analytics, edge cases, dashboards, and secondary features before defining what must be learned. The result is a larger deliverable with the same uncertainty."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"2. Calling a high-fidelity Prototype an MVP"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A prototype can look almost identical to a finished product and still test only the experience. Visual fidelity does not make an artifact an MVP."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"3. Using an MVP to answer a technical question"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"When a critical integration is unproven, discovering the problem after building the rest of the product can make the most expensive part of the project harder to change. Isolate the technical question with a focused PoC first."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"4. Making a PoC without success criteria"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"“Try the technology” is not a measurable experiment. Define the condition, threshold, test environment, and decision before you start."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"5. Trying to validate every uncertainty at once"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A single experiment becomes difficult to interpret when it contains too many questions. Separate evidence by risk so each result can change a specific decision."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"6. Measuring after the experiment instead of before it"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If the team decides what success means only after seeing the result, confirmation bias becomes easier. Predefine the signal that would support or challenge the hypothesis."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"7. Optimizing for deliverables instead of learning"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A full design system, a long feature list, or a technically impressive demo can feel like progress while leaving the main business question unresolved."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"How Kapitech Thinks About Validation"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Kapitech approaches validation as a chain that connects a risk to evidence and then to a decision. The point is not to enforce one fixed methodology. The point is to make every experiment earn its scope."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/framework.svg","alt":"Kapitech validation framework connecting business risk, user risk, technical risk, validation method, evidence, and the next decision","caption":"A practical chain: risk → validation method → evidence → decision."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Start with the business problem and the decision that matters.","Separate business, user, and technical uncertainties instead of blending them into one “product risk.”","Choose the smallest credible validation method for the highest-cost uncertainty.","Define evidence and thresholds before work begins.","Use the result to guide the next scope decision."]},{"type":"callout","title":"Kapitech principle","text":"The output of validation is not “we built it.” The output is “we know more, and we know what to do next.”","tone":"success"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Before You Build Anything"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use this checklist before committing significant design or engineering effort. The answers do not need to be perfect. They need to be explicit enough to guide the next experiment."},{"type":"checklist","title":"Validation checklist","items":["**Problem:** What specific problem are we solving, and for whom?","**User:** Who experiences the problem often enough for the solution to matter?","**Business:** What value or business assumption could be wrong?","**UX:** What part of the experience or workflow is still uncertain?","**Technology:** What technical assumption could invalidate the plan?","**Evidence:** What observable signal would support or challenge the hypothesis?","**Scope:** What is the smallest experiment that can produce that evidence?","**Decision:** What will we do if the evidence is positive, mixed, or negative?"]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Conclusion: Choose the test, not the label"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept are not competing levels of product maturity. They are different tools for reducing different uncertainties."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use a Prototype when the experience is uncertain. Use an MVP when real product value and behavior are uncertain. Use a PoC when technical feasibility is the risk that could derail the plan."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The strongest starting point is not the largest deliverable. It is the smallest credible experiment that can answer the question with the highest cost of being wrong."},{"type":"link","label":"Explore Kapitech MVP & Product Design","url":"https://kapitech.id/solutions/mvp-design"}]'::jsonb,
    cover_image='/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/hero-en.svg', tags='[{"slug":"mvp","name":"MVP"},{"slug":"prototype","name":"Prototype"},{"slug":"proof-of-concept","name":"Proof of Concept"},{"slug":"product-validation","name":"Product Validation"},{"slug":"digital-product","name":"Digital Product"},{"slug":"product-discovery","name":"Product Discovery"},{"slug":"ux-design","name":"UX Design"}]'::jsonb, reading_time=15,
    key_takeaways='["Choose a Prototype when the biggest uncertainty is whether users can understand and use the experience.","Choose an MVP when the biggest uncertainty is whether real users will adopt and value the core product.","Choose a Proof of Concept when a critical technical assumption could invalidate the wider product plan.","Prototype, MVP, and PoC are not mandatory stages. Use one or combine methods based on the risk in front of you.","Define the validation question, evidence threshold, and decision before you build the experiment.","Technical feasibility, product value, and production readiness are separate questions."]'::jsonb, faq='[{"question":"Is an MVP the same as a prototype?","answer":"No. A prototype primarily tests an experience and usability. An MVP is a working product used to learn from real behavior related to a value hypothesis."},{"question":"What is the difference between an MVP and a PoC?","answer":"An MVP tests product value and user behavior. A PoC tests whether a specific technical approach, integration, dataset, or architecture can work under defined conditions."},{"question":"When should a business build a prototype?","answer":"Build one when the main uncertainty is how users will understand, navigate, or complete the experience and changing that experience later would be costly."},{"question":"Does a prototype need a backend?","answer":"Not necessarily. Use a backend only when real system behavior is part of the question you need to validate."},{"question":"Does every product need a PoC?","answer":"No. A PoC is useful when a critical technical assumption is unproven and could materially change the product plan."},{"question":"Does an MVP have to be production-ready?","answer":"Not automatically. It should be reliable enough for the context and experiment, while broader production hardening can remain a separate decision."},{"question":"Can Prototype, MVP, and PoC be used together?","answer":"Yes. A project can combine methods when it has multiple critical uncertainties. The sequence should follow the risks being reduced."}]'::jsonb,
    seo_title='MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Which One Should Your Business Choose? | Kapitech', seo_description='Understand the difference between MVP, Prototype, and Proof of Concept, what each validates, and how to choose the right approach for your biggest product risk.', og_image='/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/hero-en.svg',
    canonical_url='https://kapitech.id/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept-guide',
    data=jsonb_set(jsonb_set(COALESCE(data,'{}'::jsonb),'{editorialMeta}','{"editorialType":"Guide"}'::jsonb,true),'{seo}','{"title":"MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Which One Should Your Business Choose? | Kapitech","description":"Understand the difference between MVP, Prototype, and Proof of Concept, what each validates, and how to choose the right approach for your biggest product risk.","slug":"mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept-guide","canonicalUrl":"https://kapitech.id/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept-guide","ogImage":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/hero-en.svg"}'::jsonb,true),
    updated_at=NOW()
WHERE slug='mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept-guide' AND language='en';

UPDATE cms_insights
SET title='MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Mana yang Tepat untuk Bisnis Anda?', excerpt='Prototype, MVP, dan Proof of Concept mengurangi jenis ketidakpastian yang berbeda. Pahami fungsi masing-masing, perbedaannya, contoh penggunaannya, dan cara memilih eksperimen yang paling tepat sebelum menghabiskan lebih banyak waktu dan anggaran.', content='[{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ketika sebuah perusahaan ingin membangun produk digital baru, pembicaraan biasanya langsung masuk ke fitur, teknologi, jadwal, dan anggaran. Semua itu penting, tetapi belum menjawab pertanyaan pertama yang seharusnya menentukan arah pekerjaan: apa yang masih bisa membuat produk ini gagal?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ketidakpastian tersebut bisa berada pada pengalaman pengguna, nilai produk, atau teknologi di baliknya. Tim bisa memiliki ide bisnis yang kuat tetapi belum tahu apakah pengguna akan memahami alurnya. Prototype bisa terlihat sangat meyakinkan tetapi belum membuktikan bahwa pelanggan nyata akan menggunakannya. Sebaliknya, arah produk bisa sudah jelas sementara integrasi, sumber data, atau kebutuhan performa yang kritis belum pernah dibuktikan."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype, MVP, dan Proof of Concept sering dianggap sebagai tiga ukuran dari produk yang sama. Padahal tidak. Masing-masing adalah alat validasi untuk menghasilkan jenis bukti yang berbeda."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Kerangka berpikir utama artikel ini sederhana: **pilih hal terkecil yang bisa menjawab ketidakpastian terbesar Anda**. Prinsip ini membuat desain dan rekayasa tetap berfokus pada keputusan, bukan sekadar menghasilkan keluaran yang lebih besar."},{"type":"callout","title":"Prinsip utama","text":"Jangan memilih metode karena labelnya terdengar lebih maju. Pilih karena metode tersebut dapat mengurangi risiko yang paling penting saat ini.","tone":"info"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Jawaban dalam 30 detik"},{"type":"table","headers":["Pendekatan","Paling tepat untuk","Pertanyaan utama","Bukti yang dicari"],"rows":[["Prototype","Risiko pengalaman","Apakah pengguna memahami dan dapat menggunakan alurnya?","Kemudahan penggunaan, keberhasilan tugas, pemahaman, masukan"],["MVP","Risiko nilai produk","Apakah pengguna nyata mau menggunakan dan menghargai solusi inti?","Penggunaan, aktivasi, penggunaan berulang, konversi, perilaku operasional"],["Proof of Concept","Risiko teknis","Apakah pendekatan teknis yang kritis benar-benar dapat bekerja?","Hasil integrasi, performa, akurasi, keandalan, batasan"]]},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/validate-id.svg","alt":"Perbandingan fokus validasi Prototype, MVP, dan Proof of Concept berdasarkan pengalaman pengguna, nilai produk, dan kelayakan teknis","caption":"Tiga pendekatan ini berbeda karena pertanyaan validasinya berbeda: pengalaman pengguna, nilai produk, dan kelayakan teknis."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Tidak ada aturan bahwa setiap produk harus selalu melewati Prototype, kemudian PoC, lalu MVP. Urutan yang tepat bergantung pada pertanyaan yang paling berisiko dan paling mahal bila ditemukan terlambat."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Apa itu Prototype?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype adalah representasi dari pengalaman yang ingin dibangun, dengan tingkat detail yang cukup untuk menguji bagaimana orang memahami, menavigasi, dan menyelesaikan tugas utama. Bentuknya bisa berupa sketsa sederhana, alur klik, atau antarmuka dengan fidelitas tinggi. Yang paling penting bukan kemewahan tampilannya, tetapi apakah prototype memungkinkan Anda mengamati perilaku yang masih belum pasti."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Apa yang divalidasi oleh Prototype?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype paling berguna ketika ketidakpastian berada pada hierarki informasi, navigasi, interaksi, alur kerja, kata-kata yang digunakan, atau cara pengguna menyelesaikan tugas. Nielsen Norman Group menjelaskan pengujian prototype sebagai pengujian kemudahan penggunaan pada representasi produk sebelum produk nyata tersedia, sehingga masalah dapat ditemukan sebelum biaya implementasi meningkat. Nielsen Norman Group menjelaskan pengujian prototype sebagai pengujian kemudahan penggunaan pada representasi produk sebelum produk nyata tersedia. Lihat [panduan pengujian prototype dari NN/g](https://media.nngroup.com/media/articles/attachments/glossary-ux-research.pdf)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype yang baik memberi bukti tentang apakah pengguna memahami alur, di titik mana mereka ragu, apa yang mereka harapkan setelah suatu tindakan, dan bagian mana yang perlu diubah."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Apa yang tidak bisa dibuktikan oleh Prototype?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype yang sangat realistis tetap tidak otomatis membuktikan adanya permintaan pasar, keandalan produksi, arsitektur backend, keamanan, atau kemampuan sistem menangani skala besar. Prototype dapat membuktikan bahwa pengalaman masuk akal tanpa membuktikan bahwa sistem lengkap di belakangnya siap."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Kapan sebaiknya membuat Prototype?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Alur utama masih sering berubah.","Tim belum yakin bagaimana pengguna akan memahami proses tersebut.","Pemangku kepentingan memiliki gambaran yang berbeda tentang cara kerja produk.","Perubahan UX setelah pengembangan akan mahal.","Anda perlu membandingkan beberapa alternatif sebelum menentukan arah implementasi."]},{"type":"callout","title":"Aturan praktis","text":"Jika risiko terbesar adalah “kita mungkin sedang merancang pengalaman yang salah”, Prototype biasanya lebih tepat daripada langsung membuat MVP.","tone":"neutral"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Apa itu MVP?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Minimum Viable Product adalah produk yang benar-benar bekerja dan sengaja dibatasi untuk menguji hipotesis nilai yang penting dengan pengguna nyata. Tujuannya bukan sekadar membuat produk yang lebih kecil. Tujuannya adalah memperoleh pembelajaran tervalidasi dari penggunaan nyata sehingga keputusan produk berikutnya bisa dibuat dengan bukti yang lebih baik."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Eric Ries menjelaskan MVP dalam konteks pembelajaran tervalidasi: tim membuat versi minimum yang diperlukan untuk belajar apakah hipotesis produk bekerja. Artinya, cakupan MVP seharusnya mengikuti kebutuhan pembelajaran, bukan jumlah fitur yang sudah disepakati sejak awal. Eric Ries menjelaskan MVP dalam konteks pembelajaran tervalidasi melalui [The Lean Startup](https://leanstartup.co/resources/articles/what-is-an-mvp/)."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/learning-loop-id.svg","alt":"Siklus MVP dari masalah dan solusi inti menuju pengguna nyata, masukan, pembelajaran, dan iterasi","caption":"MVP adalah loop pembelajaran. Produk menjadi alat untuk memperoleh bukti dan menentukan apa yang perlu dibangun berikutnya."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Apa yang divalidasi oleh MVP?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Apakah nilai inti produk menghasilkan perilaku pengguna yang bermakna.","Apakah pengguna melakukan aktivasi, menyelesaikan tugas utama, kembali menggunakan produk, atau melakukan tindakan penting lainnya.","Apakah solusi cukup bernilai untuk mengubah kebiasaan atau proses yang sudah berjalan.","Bagian mana dari alur yang benar-benar menciptakan nilai dan mana yang belum diperlukan."]},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Apa yang tidak otomatis dibuktikan oleh MVP?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"MVP dapat memberi bukti kuat bahwa sebuah solusi memiliki nilai, tetapi tetap membutuhkan pekerjaan lanjutan untuk keamanan enterprise, keandalan, skala, tooling operasional, aksesibilitas, atau kelengkapan fitur. Kesiapan produksi adalah keputusan yang berbeda dari keputusan apakah nilai inti produk layak dilanjutkan."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Kapan sebaiknya membuat MVP?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Buat MVP ketika masalah dan target pengguna sudah cukup jelas sehingga penggunaan nyata dapat menjawab pertanyaan tentang nilai. Jika tim bahkan belum tahu apakah pengguna memahami alur dasar, menguji pengalaman terlebih dahulu sering kali lebih efisien."},{"type":"callout","title":"Perlu diingat","text":"MVP harus sekecil mungkin **tanpa menjadi terlalu kecil untuk menghasilkan bukti yang dapat dipercaya**. Tujuannya bukan meminimalkan kode, tetapi meminimalkan pekerjaan yang tidak dibutuhkan untuk belajar.","tone":"info"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Apa itu Proof of Concept?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Proof of Concept, atau PoC, adalah eksperimen teknis yang sengaja dibuat fokus untuk mengetahui apakah pendekatan tertentu cukup layak untuk dilanjutkan. PoC paling berguna ketika satu ketidakpastian teknis dapat menggagalkan rencana produk yang lebih besar."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Contoh pertanyaannya: apakah API lama dapat memasok data yang dibutuhkan dalam waktu respons yang dapat diterima? Apakah layanan pihak ketiga dapat menangani pola integrasi yang diperlukan? Apakah model AI mencapai ambang akurasi minimum pada data nyata? Apakah arsitektur tertentu memenuhi batasan performa atau konkurensi yang sudah ditetapkan?"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Apa yang membuat PoC berguna?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Satu pertanyaan teknis yang jelas.","Cakupan yang sengaja sempit.","Kriteria keberhasilan dan kegagalan yang jelas.","Kondisi pengujian yang dapat diukur.","Keputusan yang akan diambil setelah hasil keluar."]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Panduan digital.gov.au memperlakukan PoC sebagai eksperimen kecil untuk menguji kelayakan sebelum investasi yang lebih besar. Artinya, PoC yang berhasil membuktikan asumsi teknis yang diuji, bukan otomatis membuktikan bahwa seluruh produk siap diluncurkan. IBM juga mendorong eksperimen terfokus ketika mengevaluasi teknologi baru dan kecocokannya dengan sistem yang sudah ada. Panduan [digital.gov.au](https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/AI-POC-to-scale/context-and-principles) menempatkan PoC sebagai eksperimen terfokus untuk menguji kelayakan sebelum investasi yang lebih besar. [IBM](https://www.ibm.com/think/insights/guide-to-experimenting-new-ai-technologies) juga mendorong eksperimen terfokus ketika mengevaluasi teknologi baru dan kecocokannya dengan sistem yang sudah ada."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Kapan sebaiknya menggunakan PoC?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Gunakan PoC ketika ketidakpastian teknis tersebut penting dan mahal jika baru ditemukan di akhir. Jika teknologinya sudah terbukti dan pertanyaan yang lebih besar justru tentang nilai produk atau pengalaman pengguna, PoC mungkin tidak memberi banyak nilai tambahan."},{"type":"quote","text":"PoC yang berhasil menjawab “apakah ini bisa bekerja dalam kondisi yang diuji?”. Ia tidak otomatis menjawab “apakah kita harus membangun seluruh produknya?”"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Perbedaan Sebenarnya antara Prototype, MVP, dan PoC"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Perbedaan paling jelas bukan pada bentuk artefaknya, tetapi pada bukti yang Anda perlukan setelah eksperimen selesai. Prototype bisa dibuat di perangkat desain, MVP bisa berisi kode produksi, dan PoC bisa hampir tidak memiliki antarmuka. Yang menentukan adalah pertanyaan yang ingin dijawab."},{"type":"table","headers":["Dimensi","Prototype","MVP","PoC"],"rows":[["Tujuan utama","Validasi pengalaman","Validasi nilai produk","Validasi kelayakan teknis"],["Pertanyaan utama","Apakah pengguna dapat menggunakannya?","Apakah pengguna akan menilai solusi ini bernilai?","Apakah pendekatan teknis ini dapat bekerja?"],["Risiko utama","Pengalaman pengguna","Adopsi dan nilai","Kegagalan teknis"],["Pengguna nyata","Bermanfaat, tetapi tidak selalu wajib","Penting untuk pengujian","Biasanya bukan fokus utama"],["Kedalaman fungsi","Sebagian besar dapat disimulasikan","Fungsi inti benar-benar bekerja","Hanya jalur teknis yang diuji perlu bekerja"],["Fidelitas UX","Rendah → Tinggi","Sedang → Tinggi","Rendah, kecuali interface adalah bagian dari uji"],["Kedalaman teknis","Rendah → Sedang","Sedang → Tinggi","Tinggi pada area yang diuji"],["Validasi bisnis","Terbatas","Tinggi","Terbatas"],["Validasi teknis","Terbatas","Sebagian","Tinggi"],["Siap produksi","Tidak","Tidak otomatis","Tidak"],["Keluaran umum","Pengalaman yang sudah diuji","Bukti dari penggunaan nyata","Bukti teknis dan batasan"],["Keputusan berikutnya","Ubah pengalaman","Lanjut, ubah, atau hentikan arah produk","Lanjutkan, ubah teknologi, ubah pendekatan, atau hentikan"]]},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/comparison-id.svg","alt":"Perbandingan Prototype, MVP, dan Proof of Concept berdasarkan tujuan, bukti, fidelitas UX, kedalaman teknis, dan kesiapan","caption":"Prototype, MVP, dan PoC mengoptimalkan jenis bukti yang berbeda. Tidak ada satu pun yang otomatis menjadi jalan pintas menuju produk siap produksi."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Kelayakan, nilai, dan kesiapan adalah tiga pertanyaan berbeda"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sebuah produk bisa layak secara teknis tetapi tidak memiliki permintaan yang berarti. Pengguna bisa menyukai alur yang dirancang tetapi biaya atau keandalannya terlalu berat untuk dioperasikan. MVP juga bisa menunjukkan nilai yang kuat sambil tetap membutuhkan penguatan besar sebelum digunakan sebagai sistem enterprise."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Dengan memisahkan ketiga pertanyaan ini, tim tidak menganggap bukti dari satu eksperimen sebagai bukti untuk semua hal."},{"type":"callout","title":"Butuh bantuan menentukan titik awal?","text":"Kapitech dapat membantu memetakan risiko produk, kebutuhan pengguna, dan batasan teknis sebelum Anda berkomitmen pada pengembangan yang lebih besar.","tone":"neutral"},{"type":"link","label":"Diskusikan Produk Anda dengan Kapitech","url":"https://kapitech.id/contact"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Cara Memilih Pendekatan yang Tepat"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Mulailah dari ketidakpastian yang paling mungkin mengubah keputusan proyek. Jangan mulai dari jenis keluaran yang paling nyaman dibuat oleh tim."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/decision-tree-id.svg","alt":"Bagan keputusan untuk memilih Prototype, MVP, atau PoC berdasarkan ketidakpastian terbesar","caption":"Mulai dari risiko yang dapat mengubah keputusan proyek. Lalu pilih eksperimen terkecil yang masih menghasilkan bukti yang dapat dipercaya."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"1. Tulis ketidakpastian sebagai pertanyaan"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ubah “produk ini berisiko” menjadi sesuatu yang dapat diuji: apakah pengguna dapat menemukan status persetujuan tanpa pelatihan? apakah tim operasi akan menggunakan alur ini setiap minggu? apakah API lama dapat mengirim data yang dibutuhkan dalam waktu respons yang ditargetkan?"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"2. Tentukan biaya jika ternyata salah"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Tidak semua ketidakpastian membutuhkan investasi yang sama. Prioritaskan hal yang dapat menghasilkan pengerjaan ulang paling besar, biaya rekayasa paling tinggi, atau keputusan produk yang salah."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"3. Pilih eksperimen terkecil yang kredibel"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Pilih Prototype, MVP, PoC, atau kombinasi yang dapat menghasilkan bukti tanpa menyelesaikan masalah yang belum perlu dijawab. Eksperimen terkecil bukan yang memiliki paling sedikit piksel atau baris kode, tetapi yang memiliki cakupan minimum untuk menghasilkan bukti yang bisa dipercaya."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"4. Tentukan bukti sebelum memulai"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Tentukan apa yang akan diamati, apa yang dianggap cukup kuat, dan hasil seperti apa yang akan membuat Anda mengubah arah. Kriteria keputusan jauh lebih berguna jika ditentukan sebelum eksperimen berjalan."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"5. Kaitkan hasil dengan keputusan"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Setiap eksperimen seharusnya menghasilkan tindakan berikutnya: lanjutkan, ubah, uji lagi, atau berhenti. Tanpa keputusan, proses validasi bisa berubah menjadi rutinitas yang mengumpulkan informasi tetapi tidak mengurangi ketidakpastian."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Contoh Nyata: Platform Operasional B2B"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Bayangkan sebuah perusahaan ingin membangun platform operasional B2B untuk persetujuan, pengelolaan alur kerja, dashboard operasional, pelaporan, dan notifikasi. Produk yang sama bisa membutuhkan ketiga metode validasi, tetapi bukan untuk alasan yang sama."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Pertanyaan A: Apakah pengguna memahami alurnya?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Tim belum yakin apakah pengguna operasi dapat memahami urutan persetujuan, menemukan status pekerjaan, dan menangani pengecualian. Prototype tepat digunakan karena ketidakpastian utama berada pada pengalaman."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Pertanyaan B: Apakah produk memberi nilai yang cukup untuk mengubah perilaku?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Alurnya sudah dapat dipahami, tetapi belum ada bukti bahwa pengguna akan kembali menggunakan sistem atau bahwa proses baru akan menggantikan pekerjaan manual. MVP lebih tepat karena pertanyaannya adalah tentang adopsi dan nilai nyata."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Pertanyaan C: Apakah API lama dapat mendukung pengalaman tersebut?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Dashboard yang dirancang bergantung pada sistem lama yang belum pernah diuji untuk pola penggunaan baru. PoC dapat mengisolasi pertanyaan tentang integrasi, waktu respons, kelengkapan data, atau konkurensi sebelum tim berkomitmen pada implementasi penuh."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Itulah mengapa ketiga metode tidak seharusnya diperlakukan sebagai tangga yang wajib dinaiki satu per satu. Satu proyek bisa membutuhkan Prototype dan PoC secara berdekatan. Proyek lain mungkin cukup dengan MVP. Urutannya harus mengikuti risiko yang ingin dikurangi."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Kesalahan Umum Saat Memilih antara MVP, Prototype, dan PoC"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"1. Membangun terlalu banyak sebelum mengajukan pertanyaan utama"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Tim sering menambahkan pengaturan, peran, analitik, berbagai kasus khusus, dashboard, dan fitur sekunder sebelum menentukan apa yang harus dipelajari. Hasilnya lebih besar, tetapi ketidakpastian utamanya tetap sama."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"2. Menganggap Prototype yang sangat detail sebagai MVP"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype bisa terlihat hampir sama seperti produk jadi tetapi tetap hanya menguji pengalaman. Tingkat kemiripan visual tidak otomatis menjadikannya MVP."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"3. Menggunakan MVP untuk menjawab pertanyaan teknis"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ketika integrasi inti belum terbukti, menemukan masalah setelah sebagian besar produk dibangun dapat membuat bagian paling mahal menjadi sulit diubah. Pisahkan pertanyaan teknis itu dengan PoC yang fokus."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"4. Membuat PoC tanpa kriteria keberhasilan"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"“Coba teknologi ini” bukan eksperimen yang cukup jelas. Tentukan kondisi uji, ambang batas, lingkungan, dan keputusan sebelum memulai."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"5. Mencoba memvalidasi semua ketidakpastian sekaligus"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Satu eksperimen menjadi sulit ditafsirkan ketika terlalu banyak pertanyaan dicampur. Pisahkan bukti berdasarkan risiko agar setiap hasil dapat mengubah keputusan tertentu."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"6. Menentukan ukuran keberhasilan setelah eksperimen selesai"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Jika tim baru menetapkan arti “berhasil” setelah melihat hasilnya, bias konfirmasi menjadi lebih mudah terjadi. Tentukan sinyal yang akan mendukung maupun menantang hipotesis sejak awal."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"7. Mengoptimalkan keluaran, bukan pembelajaran"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Design system lengkap, daftar fitur panjang, atau demo teknis yang mengesankan bisa terasa seperti kemajuan meskipun pertanyaan bisnis utama belum terjawab."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Cara Kapitech Memahami Validasi Produk"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Kapitech melihat validasi sebagai rangkaian yang menghubungkan risiko dengan bukti, lalu menghubungkan bukti dengan keputusan. Tujuannya bukan memaksakan satu metode yang selalu sama, tetapi memastikan setiap eksperimen memiliki alasan dan cakupan yang jelas."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/framework-id.svg","alt":"Kerangka validasi Kapitech yang menghubungkan risiko bisnis, risiko pengguna, risiko teknis, metode validasi, bukti, dan keputusan berikutnya","caption":"Rangkaian praktis: risiko → metode validasi → bukti → keputusan."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Mulai dari masalah bisnis dan keputusan yang benar-benar penting.","Pisahkan risiko bisnis, pengguna, dan teknis agar tidak bercampur menjadi satu “risiko produk”.","Pilih metode validasi terkecil yang kredibel untuk ketidakpastian paling mahal.","Tentukan bukti dan ambang keputusan sebelum pekerjaan dimulai.","Gunakan hasil eksperimen untuk menentukan cakupan langkah berikutnya."]},{"type":"callout","title":"Prinsip Kapitech","text":"Hasil validasi bukan “kita sudah membuatnya”. Hasil validasi adalah “kita lebih tahu, dan kita tahu apa yang harus dilakukan berikutnya”.","tone":"success"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Sebelum Anda Membangun Apa Pun"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Gunakan daftar periksa ini sebelum berkomitmen pada pekerjaan desain atau rekayasa yang besar. Jawabannya tidak harus sempurna. Yang penting, pertanyaannya cukup jelas untuk menentukan eksperimen berikutnya."},{"type":"checklist","title":"Daftar periksa validasi","items":["**Masalah:** Masalah spesifik apa yang ingin kita selesaikan, dan untuk siapa?","**Pengguna:** Siapa yang mengalami masalah tersebut cukup sering sampai solusi ini benar-benar penting?","**Bisnis:** Asumsi nilai atau bisnis apa yang bisa saja salah?","**UX:** Bagian pengalaman atau alur mana yang masih belum pasti?","**Teknologi:** Asumsi teknis apa yang dapat menggagalkan rencana?","**Bukti:** Sinyal yang dapat diamati seperti apa yang akan mendukung atau menantang hipotesis?","**Cakupan:** Apa eksperimen terkecil yang dapat menghasilkan bukti tersebut?","**Keputusan:** Apa yang akan kita lakukan jika bukti positif, campuran, atau negatif?"]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Kesimpulan: Pilih Pengujian, Bukan Label"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype, MVP, dan Proof of Concept bukan tingkat kematangan produk yang saling bersaing. Ketiganya adalah alat berbeda untuk mengurangi ketidakpastian yang berbeda."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Gunakan Prototype ketika pengalaman masih belum pasti. Gunakan MVP ketika nilai produk dan perilaku pengguna nyata masih belum terbukti. Gunakan PoC ketika kelayakan teknis adalah risiko yang dapat menggagalkan rencana."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Titik awal yang paling kuat bukanlah keluaran terbesar. Pilih eksperimen terkecil yang masih kredibel untuk menjawab pertanyaan yang paling mahal jika dibiarkan tanpa jawaban."},{"type":"link","label":"Lihat Layanan Desain MVP Kapitech","url":"https://kapitech.id/solutions/mvp-design"}]'::jsonb,
    cover_image='/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/hero.svg', tags='[{"slug":"mvp","name":"MVP"},{"slug":"prototype","name":"Prototype"},{"slug":"proof-of-concept","name":"Proof of Concept"},{"slug":"product-validation","name":"Validasi Produk"},{"slug":"digital-product","name":"Produk Digital"},{"slug":"product-discovery","name":"Penemuan Produk"},{"slug":"ux-design","name":"Desain UX"}]'::jsonb, reading_time=14,
    key_takeaways='["Gunakan Prototype ketika ketidakpastian terbesar berada pada pengalaman dan cara pengguna memahami alur.","Gunakan MVP ketika ketidakpastian terbesar berada pada nilai produk dan perilaku pengguna nyata.","Gunakan Proof of Concept ketika asumsi teknis yang kritis belum terbukti.","Prototype, MVP, dan PoC bukan tahapan wajib. Pilih atau gabungkan metode berdasarkan risiko yang sedang dihadapi.","Tentukan pertanyaan validasi, bukti yang dicari, dan keputusan sebelum eksperimen dimulai.","Kelayakan teknis, nilai produk, dan kesiapan produksi adalah tiga pertanyaan yang berbeda."]'::jsonb, faq='[{"question":"Apakah MVP sama dengan Prototype?","answer":"Tidak. Prototype terutama digunakan untuk menguji pengalaman dan kemudahan penggunaan. MVP adalah produk yang benar-benar bekerja dan digunakan untuk memperoleh pembelajaran dari perilaku nyata yang terkait dengan hipotesis nilai."},{"question":"Apa perbedaan MVP dan PoC?","answer":"MVP menguji nilai produk dan perilaku pengguna. PoC menguji apakah pendekatan teknis, integrasi, data, atau arsitektur tertentu dapat bekerja dalam kondisi yang sudah ditentukan."},{"question":"Kapan bisnis sebaiknya membuat Prototype?","answer":"Buat Prototype ketika ketidakpastian utama berada pada cara pengguna memahami, menavigasi, atau menyelesaikan pengalaman dan perubahan setelah pengembangan akan lebih mahal."},{"question":"Apakah Prototype harus memiliki backend?","answer":"Tidak selalu. Backend hanya diperlukan jika perilaku sistem nyata merupakan bagian dari pertanyaan yang ingin divalidasi."},{"question":"Apakah semua produk membutuhkan PoC?","answer":"Tidak. PoC berguna ketika asumsi teknis yang kritis belum terbukti dan hasilnya dapat mengubah rencana produk secara material."},{"question":"Apakah MVP harus siap produksi?","answer":"Tidak otomatis. MVP harus cukup andal untuk konteks dan eksperimen yang dijalankan, sementara penguatan produksi yang lebih luas bisa menjadi keputusan terpisah."},{"question":"Bisakah Prototype, MVP, dan PoC digunakan bersama?","answer":"Bisa. Satu proyek dapat menggabungkan beberapa metode ketika memiliki beberapa ketidakpastian kritis. Urutannya harus mengikuti risiko yang sedang dikurangi."}]'::jsonb,
    seo_title='MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Mana yang Tepat untuk Bisnis Anda? | Kapitech', seo_description='Pahami perbedaan MVP, Prototype, dan Proof of Concept (PoC), risiko yang diuji, contoh penggunaannya, dan cara memilih pendekatan yang tepat untuk produk digital.', og_image='/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/hero.svg',
    canonical_url='https://kapitech.id/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept',
    data=jsonb_set(jsonb_set(COALESCE(data,'{}'::jsonb),'{editorialMeta}','{"editorialType":"Panduan"}'::jsonb,true),'{seo}','{"title":"MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Mana yang Tepat untuk Bisnis Anda? | Kapitech","description":"Pahami perbedaan MVP, Prototype, dan Proof of Concept (PoC), risiko yang diuji, contoh penggunaannya, dan cara memilih pendekatan yang tepat untuk produk digital.","slug":"mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept","canonicalUrl":"https://kapitech.id/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept","ogImage":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/hero.svg"}'::jsonb,true),
